  "slug": "association-adjusting-2e22",
  "summary": "New mediation, family-based trait-disease testing, and precision matrix benchmarking address inference under high dimensionality and correlated data.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a focus on statistical inference when data are high-dimensional or correlated. LaMe uses a joint linear-Gaussian latent mediation model to estimate natural direct and indirect effects in high-dimensional exposure-mediator-outcome systems. QT-RelCor provides parametric and non-parametric tests linking quantitative traits to binary disease status in clustered family data, where relatedness violates independence. A benchmark of precision matrix estimation methods evaluates differential co-expression network analysis on simulated and real transcriptomic data. The previous emphasis on covariate, medication, and LD-reference corrections in GWAS is not carried by these items. What is new here is the shift toward modelling dependence directly: latent mediators, family clustering, and conditional-dependence networks, with one empirical comparison of estimators.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
